PREAMBLE

This license governs the use, modification, and distribution of
Pixelian (the "Software"), the Pixel Family: a source-available
frontend template for PHP projects authored by Antranik Agopian.

It is designed to permit free personal and commercial use, modification,
and distribution while protecting the Software's identity, preserving
its original authorship, and requiring visible attribution.

This license is not a template for your own projects. It governs the
Software and its incorporated components, not independently authored
applications built using it.

Applications built with the Software may be named, distributed, and
licensed however their authors choose, subject to the requirements
applicable to the incorporated Software.


1. PERMISSION

You are granted a worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive, perpetual
license, subject to the conditions below, to:

  a. Use the Software for any purpose, including commercial purposes.
  b. Copy and distribute the Software, in source or compiled form.
  c. Modify the Software and create derivative works.
  d. Incorporate the Software into larger applications and distribute
     those applications under licensing terms of your choice.

The permissions in section 1.d do not grant permission to relicense
the Software itself, remove its existing license conditions, or
redistribute it as an independently branded replacement framework.

The permissions are conditioned on compliance with sections 2, 4,
5, and 6 of this license.

No express patent license is granted by this license.


2. ATTRIBUTION

When you copy, modify, or distribute the Software, you must comply
with the following requirements.

  a. COPYRIGHT AND SOURCE HEADERS

     Retain the original copyright and license notices in all copies
     of the Software.

     Original source files containing "@copyright", "@license", or
     "@author" lines must retain those lines unchanged, whether the
     files are redistributed with or without modifications.

     You may add additional notices identifying your own contributions,
     provided they do not replace, obscure, or misrepresent the
     original authorship.

  b. LICENSE PRESERVATION

     Retain this LICENSE file, or its complete text, with any source
     or compiled distribution containing the Software.

     When the Software is distributed as part of a larger application,
     its license may be included in a third-party licenses or notices
     directory.

     This requirement does not make the Pixelian License the license
     of the independently authored application.

     For hosted applications where the Software itself is not
     distributed to recipients, the runtime attribution requirements
     in section 2.d still apply.

  c. MODIFICATION HEADERS

     When modifying an original code file distributed with the
     Software, you must:

       - Retain its original "@copyright" line.
       - Retain its original "@license" line.
       - Retain its original "@author" line, where present.
       - Add a "@modified-by" line identifying the modifier.

     The "@modified-by" line must follow the format documented in
     docs/standards.md.

     Additional modifiers may add their own "@modified-by" lines
     without removing previous entries.

     These requirements apply to original executable source files,
     including JavaScript, CSS, and PHP files.

     They also apply to files containing copied and modified Pixelian
     source code, where the file format supports source comments.

     Configuration files and other non-code files that do not support
     such headers are exempt from the modification-header requirement.

     Modifying code without adding the required "@modified-by" line
     constitutes a license violation. The absence of this line does
     not qualify modified code for unmodified attribution.

  d. RUNTIME ATTRIBUTION

     Any application distributed or deployed for use by others that
     incorporates a substantial portion of the Software must display
     a clear attribution to Pixelian.dev.

     A substantial portion means original Pixelian code, styling,
     layout components, modules, or core primitives that perform a
     material functional or structural role in the application.

     Incidental use of a small, isolated code fragment that does not
     materially incorporate the Software's functionality or structure
     does not, by itself, trigger runtime attribution.

     Attribution must appear in a location that a typical user
     viewing the application could reach within two clicks, such as
     the footer of any page or an About screen linked from the main
     navigation.

     Where hyperlinks are supported, "Pixelian.dev" must link to
     https://pixelian.dev.

     If the application has no user-facing interface, equivalent
     attribution must appear in its accompanying documentation or
     distribution notices.

     The required attribution depends on whether the Software's
     original code has been modified.


     UNMODIFIED CODE

     If the application incorporates original Pixelian code without
     modifying that code, the attribution must read:

       "Powered by Pixelian.dev"

     The default attribution shipped in data/site.json satisfies
     this requirement.

     The following do not count as Code Modifications:

       - Configuration changes to data/*.json.
       - Changes to robots.txt, sitemap.xml, or site.webmanifest.
       - Placeholder substitutions performed by setup.sh.
       - Adding independently authored modules under js/modules/.
       - Adding independently authored CSS or JavaScript files.
       - Adding new data or configuration files.
       - Building application-specific functionality without altering
         or adapting existing Pixelian source code.

     These activities preserve the unmodified attribution tier,
     provided no original Pixelian code is otherwise modified.


     MODIFIED CODE

     If the application incorporates modifications to original
     Pixelian code, the attribution must read:

       "Extends Pixelian.dev"

     An equivalent phrase is permitted if it clearly identifies
     Pixelian.dev as the source of the extended work and does not
     imply that the copyright holder authored the application.

     A Code Modification includes:

       - Editing an original JavaScript, CSS, or PHP code file.
       - Removing or replacing original executable code.
       - Changing original layout, styling, modules, or core logic.
       - Copying original Pixelian code into another file and
         modifying that copied code.

     The size of the modification does not matter.

     Changing even one line in an original stylesheet triggers
     the modified attribution tier.

     Attribution is determined by actual code modifications, not
     solely by the presence or absence of "@modified-by" annotations.

     Removing or failing to add a required annotation does not
     exempt the application from the modified attribution tier.


     GENERAL ATTRIBUTION RULES

     In both tiers:

       - Attribution must identify Pixelian.dev as the source.
       - Attribution must be visible or reasonably discoverable.
       - Attribution must not be deliberately hidden or obscured.
       - Attribution must not imply personal authorship or endorsement
         by Antranik Agopian.
       - Equivalent replacement text must preserve the same meaning.

     Removing or obscuring the required attribution without an
     equivalent replacement is a violation of this license.


3. APPLICATIONS BUILT WITH THE SOFTWARE

Applications developed using the Software may be named, sold,
distributed, and licensed however their authors choose.

This license does not require application authors to:

  a. Publish their independently authored source code.
  b. License their independently authored code under this license.
  c. Use the Pixelian name as their application's name.
  d. Obtain permission for ordinary commercial application use.

However, original Pixelian code incorporated into an application
remains governed by the Pixelian License.

The application's independent code and the incorporated Software
may therefore be subject to different licensing terms.

The attribution requirements in section 2 apply to qualifying
applications, including commercially distributed applications.

Including the Pixelian License in third-party notices does not
make that license applicable to independently authored application
code.

An application must not be presented as an independently developed
replacement framework when its primary purpose is to redistribute
Pixelian or a modified version of Pixelian as a reusable template,
starter kit, or framework.

Such distributions are governed by the name-preservation requirements
in section 4.


4. NAME PRESERVATION

The Software must continue to be identified as "Pixelian".

Forks, modified versions, and derivative distributions of the Software
must preserve the Pixelian identity when distributed as reusable
frameworks, frontend templates, boilerplates, or starter kits.

You may not:

  a. Rename the Software to an unrelated framework name.
  b. Redistribute Pixelian under a different identity while concealing
     or replacing its original project name.
  c. Present modified Pixelian code as an independently authored
     replacement framework without identifying its Pixelian origin.
  d. Remove the Pixelian identity from a fork to imply that the
     underlying framework was developed independently.

Unofficial forks may use descriptive qualifiers, such as:

  "Pixelian - Unofficial Fork"
  "Pixelian - Community Edition"
  "Pixelian (Modified by [Contributor])"

Such identifiers must clearly distinguish the fork from the official
Pixelian project.

Forks must not claim to be official releases or imply endorsement,
maintenance, or supervision by the original copyright holder without
written permission.

This requirement applies to the Software itself, not to applications
built using the Software.

Applications may use any name or brand chosen by their authors,
provided they comply with sections 2 and 5.

The name-preservation requirement does not prevent developers from
creating original frameworks inspired by general ideas or concepts
used in Pixelian, provided they do not reproduce protected Pixelian
code in violation of this license.


5. TRADEMARK AND BRAND PROTECTION

The names "Antranik", "Antranik Agopian", "Antranik.dev", and
"Antranik.am" identify the copyright holder and author.

The names "Pixelian", "Pixelian.dev", and "Pixelian.net" identify
the Software and its project.

These names, together with their associated logos and branding,
remain subject to the applicable rights of their respective owners.

No general trademark license is granted.

You may not use these names or associated logos as the primary brand
of an unrelated product, service, or application without written
permission from the applicable rights holder.

Requests for written permission should be sent to hello@antranik.dev.

Limited use of the Pixelian name is permitted when necessary to:

  a. Identify the original Software.
  b. Fulfill the attribution requirements in section 2.
  c. Preserve the Software's name under section 4.
  d. Truthfully describe compatibility with or derivation from
     the Software.

An unofficial fork may use the Pixelian name as part of its required
framework identification, provided it is clearly identified as
unofficial and does not impersonate the official project.

Such permitted identification does not grant permission to use
official project logos, falsely claim endorsement, or present a
modified distribution as an official Pixelian release.


